WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.113
In part one we traced insertion sort

00:00:03.113 --> 00:00:05.470
and it worked on that example.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.979
But does it work on every input?

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:10.031
To know that, we prove it

00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:11.780
with a loop invariant.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.085
A loop invariant is a statement about the

00:00:15.157 --> 00:00:18.184
variables that is true every time the loop

00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:20.490
is about to test its condition.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.277
For insertion sort

00:00:22.277 --> 00:00:25.151
in the textbook's numbering from one

00:00:25.151 --> 00:00:28.025
the invariant of the outer loop says

00:00:28.025 --> 00:00:30.445
at the start of each iteration

00:00:30.521 --> 00:00:33.773
the subarray A[1..j-1] holds the

00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:36.572
elements that were originally there

00:00:36.572 --> 00:00:38.160
but in sorted order.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.375
A loop-invariant proof has three parts.

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:44.415
Initialization: the invariant is true before

00:00:44.482 --> 00:00:45.833
the first iteration.

00:00:45.833 --> 00:00:46.712
Maintenance

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:49.076
if it is true before an iteration

00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:51.643
it is still true before the next one.

00:00:51.643 --> 00:00:54.210
And termination: when the loop stops

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.980
the invariant tells us something useful.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.893
This is like mathematical induction.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:02.242
Initialization is the base case

00:01:02.242 --> 00:01:05.018
and maintenance is the inductive step.

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.368
The difference is that induction

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:10.002
goes on forever, while a loop stops

00:01:10.073 --> 00:01:11.781
and termination is where

00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:13.490
the invariant pays off.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.429
Initialization.

00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:18.872
Before the first iteration, j is 2

00:01:18.872 --> 00:01:21.862
so the subarray is A[1..1]

00:01:21.862 --> 00:01:23.493
a single element.

00:01:23.493 --> 00:01:26.030
It is the original element

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.110
and one element is always sorted.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.460
Maintenance.

00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:33.709
Suppose A[1..j-1] holds the

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.792
original first j-1 elements

00:01:36.792 --> 00:01:38.209
in sorted order.

00:01:38.209 --> 00:01:41.042
The body takes key = A[j]

00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:44.624
moves the larger elements one place right

00:01:44.708 --> 00:01:47.207
and stops at the first element

00:01:47.291 --> 00:01:50.540
that is not larger, or at the left end.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:53.233
It writes key into the hole.

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:56.495
Everything to its left is at most key

00:01:56.495 --> 00:02:00.093
everything to its right, up to position j

00:02:00.093 --> 00:02:01.850
is greater than key

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.192
and still in its old order.

00:02:04.276 --> 00:02:07.288
So A[1..j] now holds the original

00:02:07.372 --> 00:02:10.133
first j elements in sorted order

00:02:10.133 --> 00:02:13.730
and incrementing j restores the invariant.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.111
Termination.

00:02:15.111 --> 00:02:18.807
The for loop stops when j first exceeds n

00:02:18.807 --> 00:02:21.815
that is, when j equals n + 1.

00:02:21.815 --> 00:02:25.339
Put that into the invariant: A[1..n]

00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:26.800
the whole array

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:30.668
holds the original elements in sorted order.

00:02:30.668 --> 00:02:33.160
So the algorithm is correct.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.370
A common mistake

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.246
the loop stops at j equals n.

00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:39.493
It does not.

00:02:39.493 --> 00:02:43.520
The iteration with j equals n still runs

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.300
it inserts the last element.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:49.986
If you put j equals n into the invariant

00:02:50.067 --> 00:02:52.345
you only learn that

00:02:52.426 --> 00:02:54.216
A[1..n-1] is sorted

00:02:54.216 --> 00:02:57.633
and here the 3 is still out of place.

00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:01.050
The test fails only when j is n + 1.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.304
Another mistake is to forget the words

00:03:04.304 --> 00:03:06.016
the original elements.

00:03:06.016 --> 00:03:08.920
A[1..j-1] is sorted, alone

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:09.888
is too weak

00:03:09.963 --> 00:03:12.717
an algorithm that overwrote the whole

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.472
array with zeros would keep it true.

00:03:15.547 --> 00:03:18.897
The invariant must also say that the elements

00:03:18.972 --> 00:03:21.280
are a permutation of the input.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.016
You can even check an invariant

00:03:24.085 --> 00:03:25.546
while a program runs.

00:03:25.615 --> 00:03:28.188
This program asserts it at the top of

00:03:28.258 --> 00:03:30.761
every iteration of the Java version

00:03:30.761 --> 00:03:32.500
which numbers from 0.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.012
Before j equals 1

00:03:35.012 --> 00:03:37.715
the prefix is just 5.

00:03:37.715 --> 00:03:39.445
Then 2, 5.

00:03:39.445 --> 00:03:41.823
Then 2, 4, 5.

00:03:41.823 --> 00:03:44.742
Then 2, 4, 5, 6

00:03:44.742 --> 00:03:48.850
and every time the invariant is true.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.693
The loop ends with j equal to 6

00:03:51.693 --> 00:03:53.527
the length of the array

00:03:53.527 --> 00:03:55.800
and the whole array is sorted.

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.388
Now the third question

00:03:58.388 --> 00:04:00.396
how long does it take?

00:04:00.396 --> 00:04:01.269
To count

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:04.762
we need a simple model of the computer

00:04:04.762 --> 00:04:08.341
the random-access machine, or RAM model.

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:11.485
Instructions run one after another

00:04:11.485 --> 00:04:13.580
never at the same time.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:16.177
Each basic operation takes a

00:04:16.257 --> 00:04:19.146
constant amount of time: arithmetic

00:04:19.146 --> 00:04:21.955
loading and storing, a comparison

00:04:21.955 --> 00:04:23.319
a function call

00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:26.690
and reading the i-th element of an array.

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.258
The model ignores caches and the

00:04:29.327 --> 00:04:31.059
details of the processor.

00:04:31.059 --> 00:04:32.792
Those matter for tuning

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:36.049
but they rarely change which of two algorithms

00:04:36.118 --> 00:04:37.920
is faster on large inputs.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.690
The running time is the number

00:04:40.763 --> 00:04:42.077
of steps executed.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.829
One execution of a line

00:04:43.902 --> 00:04:45.508
takes a constant time

00:04:45.508 --> 00:04:47.624
so we give each line a cost

00:04:47.624 --> 00:04:48.938
c1 to c8

00:04:48.938 --> 00:04:51.420
and count how many times it runs.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.852
The key quantity is t_j

00:04:53.939 --> 00:04:56.889
the number of times the while test

00:04:56.975 --> 00:04:59.491
on line five runs for that j.

00:04:59.491 --> 00:05:02.614
The test runs once for every shift

00:05:02.614 --> 00:05:06.085
plus once more for the test that fails

00:05:06.085 --> 00:05:09.034
so the body, lines six and seven

00:05:09.034 --> 00:05:11.290
runs t_j - 1 times.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.075
Line one runs n times, not n - 1

00:05:15.075 --> 00:05:17.277
the test runs once more

00:05:17.277 --> 00:05:21.240
with j equal to n + 1, to end the loop.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.788
Adding cost times times over all the

00:05:24.877 --> 00:05:28.164
lines gives the running time, T(n).

00:05:28.252 --> 00:05:30.384
It depends not only on n

00:05:30.473 --> 00:05:32.250
but also on the t_j

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:35.270
on which input of size n we sort.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:39.031
Best case: the array is already sorted.

00:05:39.113 --> 00:05:41.560
Every key is at least as large

00:05:41.642 --> 00:05:43.681
as the element before it

00:05:43.681 --> 00:05:46.944
so the while test fails the first time

00:05:46.944 --> 00:05:47.923
t_j is 1

00:05:47.923 --> 00:05:50.778
and lines six and seven never run.

00:05:50.778 --> 00:05:52.572
T(n) is an + b

00:05:52.572 --> 00:05:54.530
a linear function of n.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.537
Worst case: the array is sorted in reverse.

00:05:58.622 --> 00:06:01.089
Every key is smaller than all

00:06:01.174 --> 00:06:03.130
the elements before it

00:06:03.215 --> 00:06:05.427
so it is compared with all

00:06:05.512 --> 00:06:07.213
j - 1 of them

00:06:07.213 --> 00:06:10.785
and then with the left end: t_j equals j.

00:06:10.785 --> 00:06:14.613
The sums become n(n + 1)/2 - 1

00:06:14.613 --> 00:06:18.440
and n(n - 1)/2.

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:21.490
Substituting gives an^2

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.020
+ bn + c.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:25.630
The worst case is quadratic.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.035
When we talk about the best or worst case

00:06:29.035 --> 00:06:31.289
the algorithm does not change.

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:33.471
It still handles every input

00:06:33.544 --> 00:06:35.653
the case only describes which

00:06:35.726 --> 00:06:37.180
inputs make it fast.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:38.595
Your turn.

00:06:38.595 --> 00:06:41.766
For a reversed array of 6 elements

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:44.270
how many times does the while

00:06:44.354 --> 00:06:45.856
test run in total

00:06:45.856 --> 00:06:48.360
and how many shifts are made?

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:51.131
And for a sorted array of 6?

00:06:51.131 --> 00:06:53.310
Pause the video and count.

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:59.215
Here it is, pass by pass.

00:06:59.215 --> 00:07:03.306
The first pass tests twice and shifts once.

00:07:03.306 --> 00:07:07.490
The next tests three times and shifts twice.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.807
Then 4 tests and 3 shifts

00:07:10.807 --> 00:07:13.324
5 tests and 4 shifts

00:07:13.324 --> 00:07:16.111
and 6 tests and 5 shifts.

00:07:16.111 --> 00:07:20.067
In total, 20 tests, and 15 shifts

00:07:20.067 --> 00:07:23.393
6 * 7 / 2 - 1

00:07:23.393 --> 00:07:26.000
and 6 * 5 / 2.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.118
A sorted array of 6 makes n - 1

00:07:30.118 --> 00:07:33.321
5 tests, and no shifts at all.

00:07:33.415 --> 00:07:35.770
The program InsertionCost

00:07:35.864 --> 00:07:38.973
counts them for real, for sorted

00:07:38.973 --> 00:07:42.270
reversed and pseudo-random arrays.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.270
The first row is our 6: 5 / 0

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.140
20 / 15.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.651
For 10000 elements

00:07:50.734 --> 00:07:54.307
the reversed array takes 50004999

00:07:54.391 --> 00:07:57.715
tests

00:07:57.715 --> 00:08:00.790
exactly n(n + 1)/2

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:01.704
minus 1.

00:08:01.787 --> 00:08:04.280
And the random column is close

00:08:04.363 --> 00:08:06.690
to half of the reversed one.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:10.574
Multiplying n by 10 multiplies the reversed

00:08:10.655 --> 00:08:13.627
and random counts by about 100.

00:08:13.627 --> 00:08:17.000
That is what quadratic growth looks like.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:19.808
Which case do we analyse?

00:08:19.808 --> 00:08:21.947
Usually the worst case.

00:08:21.947 --> 00:08:24.799
It is a guaranteed upper bound

00:08:24.799 --> 00:08:27.740
no input of size n takes longer.

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:30.343
For some algorithms the worst

00:08:30.421 --> 00:08:31.819
case occurs often.

00:08:31.897 --> 00:08:34.461
Searching for an item that is not

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:36.714
there always costs the most

00:08:36.714 --> 00:08:40.210
and in many applications most searches fail.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.365
And the average case is often about

00:08:43.445 --> 00:08:45.448
as bad as the worst case.

00:08:45.448 --> 00:08:46.810
On random input

00:08:46.891 --> 00:08:49.695
key is on average larger than about

00:08:49.775 --> 00:08:52.259
half of the elements before it

00:08:52.259 --> 00:08:54.503
so t_j is about j over two.

00:08:54.503 --> 00:08:56.907
That halves the leading term

00:08:56.907 --> 00:08:59.071
but it is still quadratic.

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:01.875
The best case is the least useful

00:09:01.875 --> 00:09:04.920
it describes only the luckiest input.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:06.357
To sum up.

00:09:06.357 --> 00:09:10.211
One: a loop invariant proves a loop correct

00:09:10.211 --> 00:09:12.010
with initialization

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.493
maintenance and termination.

00:09:14.493 --> 00:09:16.463
Two: in the RAM model

00:09:16.549 --> 00:09:18.861
the running time is the sum

00:09:18.947 --> 00:09:20.660
of cost times times.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:23.393
Three: insertion sort takes linear

00:09:23.463 --> 00:09:25.075
time on a sorted array

00:09:25.075 --> 00:09:27.738
and quadratic time on a reversed one

00:09:27.738 --> 00:09:29.490
which is the worst case.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.200
Try this yourself

00:09:31.275 --> 00:09:33.239
write a loop invariant for

00:09:33.314 --> 00:09:34.900
the inner while loop.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.997
Use it to show that when the loop stops

00:09:37.997 --> 00:09:41.320
a[i + 1] is the right place for key.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.969
In part three: recursion

00:09:43.969 --> 00:09:46.176
and divide-and-conquer

00:09:46.176 --> 00:09:48.750
the idea behind merge sort.

